{#
  Why a line is being sold or billed for more than the region has available (#326).

  One partial, included from every place a sell-side quantity box is rendered, so the control cannot
  differ between the order form and the standalone invoice form the way two hand-written copies
  eventually would. The receiving form's `_short_dated_reason.html.twig` is the same partial for the
  same job at the dock, and this deliberately copies it rather than inventing a second mechanism.

  ## Where it is included from, and why that is one place and not three

  The quantity box is itself one shared partial now — `admin/_partials/sales_line_row.html.twig` —
  so this is included from the END OF ITS QUANTITY CELL and from nowhere else on a rendered row. The
  cell is the only thing on the page that already knows this row's posted index, and the reason is
  per LINE: it has to post as `lines[N][stock_override_reason]` beside the `lines[N][qty]` it
  explains, or the server pairs a decision with the wrong figure. The shared row renders it only
  where the caller passes `showsStockOverride`, which is the order form and the standalone invoice
  create screen — the two whose saves read it.

  The order form's two row <template>s carry a copy through this same partial, because app.js builds
  a row from them rather than from the server, and a line typed with scripting on is otherwise the
  one line an admin cannot explain. `__INDEX__` is substituted globally, so the box lands under the
  same index as the quantity beside it.

  It is a text box and not a checkbox, on purpose. A tick records that somebody clicked. The only
  question anybody asks about an oversell a fortnight later is on what grounds it was taken, and a
  tick answers none of it — which is exactly the difference between an override and a shrug.

  **It is not an accusation.** Selling past the shelf is the operator's call: they know about the
  delivery landing Friday, the drop-ship, the substitution. The save is not refused because the
  decision is doubted, it waits because the RECORD is the point, and a record with an empty reason
  on it is the same as no record while looking like one.

  StockOverrideReasons reads this box, treating blank and whitespace as absent;
  StockOverrideRecorder refuses to write a row without one and writes
  `sales_order_line_stock_override` / `invoice_line_stock_override` — with this text, the person, and
  the figures the decision was measured against — when there is one. The decision then appears on
  the document, under "Sold beyond stock" / "Billed beyond stock".

  No `required` attribute, for the same reason nothing else on these forms has one: the line may be
  comfortably within stock, and `required` cannot say "only if" without JavaScript.

  Parameters:
    name  the posted field name — `lines[7][stock_override_reason]`. One spelling on both screens
          now: the standalone invoice form's parallel `line_*[]` arrays were replaced by the order
          form's indexed rows, which `AbstractAdminController::postedLineRows()` now reads for
          every sell-side screen.
    id    the element id to anchor to
    value what was typed last time, so a refused save comes back with it still in the box. The
          standalone invoice screen re-renders from the post and passes it; the order form re-renders
          from the SAVED order and has nothing to pass, so its box comes back empty — see the note
          in sales_line_row.html.twig.
#}
<br><input type="text" id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}" maxlength="255"
           value="{{ value|default('') }}" placeholder="Reason, if this goes beyond available stock">
